For each pair, choose the measurement type more useful for predicting whether
absolute oral bioavailability is at least 20% under the reported condition.

Both candidates belong to the same progressive evidence level. Judge endpoint,
assay design, biological system, route, and mechanism. Hold chemical similarity,
assay-transfer reliability, evidence quality, and evidence quantity equal.

Rules:
- Judge measurement informativeness, not whether results are positive or negative.
  Observed values and directions are withheld.
- Source indicates provenance, not usefulness. Direct-sounding wording does not
  automatically make a measurement direct F >= 20% evidence.
- Up to five diverse samples clarify each bucket. Do not reward repetition,
  sample count, or verbosity. Missing context remains unknown.
- Evaluate each pair independently. Candidate order is irrelevant. Choose one
  candidate even when the advantage is small; no ties.

